# @CHECKER@

A proof checker for the **@CALCULUS@** calculus.

Generated by Eudaimonia, a template for verified SMT proof checkers. This
directory is a complete Lake project and does not depend on the generator: it
can be moved anywhere, or made a repository of its own.

## Layout

```text
lakefile.toml            the package: the @CALCULUS@ library and the @EXE@ executable
lean-toolchain           the pinned Lean version
Main.lean                the executable: read a proof, report a verdict
@CALCULUS@.lean          the root of the library
@CALCULUS@/
  SmtEval.lean           G  the primitives the embedding is evaluated over
  @CHECKER@Term.lean     G  the term datatype of the calculus
  SmtModelDefs.lean      G  what the model semantics is built from
  SmtValueOrder.lean     G  the order on values
  SmtModel.lean          G  the model semantics of SMT-LIB
  Spec.lean              G  @CALCULUS@ <-> SMT-LIB, and satisfiability
  @CHECKER@.lean         G  the core checker
  Parser.lean            G  the operator table for this signature
  Api.lean               H  what the executable does with a file
  ApiChecks.lean         H  each check is the component it stands for
  ApiCorrect.lean        H  the soundness theorem, about the text of a file
  Diagnostics.lean       H  why a run came back incomplete
  Proofs/
    Assumptions.lean     H  the side conditions, and deciding them
    CheckerCore.lean     H  correctness of the core, rule-agnostic
    RuleLemmas.lean      G  the dispatcher over the rules
    Checker.lean         H  the soundness theorem
    Rules/               one file per rule: statement generated, proof yours
signature/               what the calculus is, and what it means
  @CALCULUS@.eo          the Eunoia signature
  @CALCULUS@.eos         what its symbols mean
  smt.eos                the SMT-LIB semantics that is written against
```

**G** is generated from the signature and is overwritten when one is installed;
**H** is hand-written and left alone. Each file says which it is in its own
header. `Proofs/Rules/` is both: a rule's statement is generated with `sorry`
for its proof, the proof goes in that same file, and a reinstall preserves it.

## What @CALCULUS@ must provide

The correctness development is not neutral about vocabulary: it is stated in
terms of specific operators, referenced as constructors of the `UserOp` enum
that `signature/@CALCULUS@.eo` compiles to. A signature that does not declare
them under these names does not lose a feature — the core proofs stop
typechecking.

| symbol | arity | why the core needs it |
| ------ | ----- | --------------------- |
| `and` | binary | the soundness statement is about the conjunction of the assumptions |
| `imp` | binary | discharging an assumption (`scope`) yields an implication |
| `not`, `eq` | binary | the shared lemma base the rule proofs draw on |
| `Bool` | type | every assumption and proven term must have EO type `Bool` |
| `true`, `false` | literals | what a proof entails, and what a refutation reaches |

The right name is necessary but not sufficient: each must also **mean** what
the SMT-LIB semantics says. A signature whose `and` is not conjunction compiles
and then fails to prove.

## Building

```bash
lake build
```

That builds the library and the executable. Either alone:

```bash
lake build @CALCULUS@     # the library
lake build @EXE@          # the executable
```

`@CALCULUS@.ApiCorrect` is deliberately outside the default target: it depends
on the whole rule-correctness tree, which is far too expensive to build every
time. Build it, and so the proofs, explicitly:

```bash
lake build @CALCULUS@.ApiCorrect
```

## Running

```bash
lake exe @EXE@ <proof>
```

After a build the executable can be run directly, without Lake:

```bash
./.lake/build/bin/@EXE@ <proof>
```

## Status

The build infrastructure and the shape of the checker are in place; the content
is not. Every module under `@CALCULUS@/` is a stub saying what belongs in it,
and `@EXE@` reports that it has nothing to check with.

What replaces the stubs is compiled from `signature/`. The Eunoia compiler that
does that is not wired up yet — see the Eudaimonia roadmap, `TODO.md`, for what
is missing and in what order it is worth building.
